=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is the crash of r1's OSPF daemon (ospfd). r1 is Kath1's
Internet edge router: its frr.conf carries a full OSPF configuration
that announces the two WAN stub links (10.0.0.32/30 toward CSW1,
10.0.0.36/30 toward CSW2) into OSPF area 0 and, critically, includes
"default-information originate" to flood the ISP-learned default route
(0.0.0.0/0) throughout the campus routing domain. At 22:27:09, roughly
90 seconds after lab boot, ospfd on r1 encountered a fatal error and
exited. Zebra on r1 logged: "Client 'ospf' encountered an error and is
shutting down" and immediately withdrew all 30 OSPF routes from r1's
own RIB. The process list on r1 confirms no ospfd is running; only
zebra, mgmtd, and staticd remain. CSW1 (which had just completed OSPF
Exchange with r1 — "Neighbor 10.0.0.76 Negotiation done" logged at
22:26:29) lost the adjacency once r1's dead timer expired, and r1
disappeared from CSW1's neighbor table. Because r1 was the sole default
originator, every campus router (dswa1, dswa2, CSW1, CSW2, etc.) is
now without a default route; CSW1's FIB has no 0.0.0.0/0 entry.
Packets from PC1 (10.1.0.100) toward the Internet reach dswa1 but
dswa1 returns ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" — confirmed by live
ping. Intra-campus reachability is unaffected: PC1 successfully pings
Office B hosts (10.3.0.100, 10.5.0.1) because all LAN prefixes are
distributed by the remaining OSPF nodes, none of which depend on r1.
The team's observation that "CSW1's OSPF neighbor went down" is correct
as a symptom but misattributed — CSW1 is fully healthy. The root cause
is ospfd crashing on r1. Fix: restart FRR on r1 ("service frr restart"
or "systemctl restart frr"); the existing frr.conf will re-establish
the OSPF adjacency with CSW1 and CSW2, re-originate the default route,
and restore Internet access for all campus devices within one OSPF
convergence cycle.
=== END ===
